    "lawCode": "Amd §3-400, El L; amd §§132 & 143, Lab L; amd §§2025, 2036 & 2607, rpld & add §3207-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5172\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the election law, the labor law, and\nthe education law, in relation to authorizing persons seventeen years\nof age to serve as election inspectors and poll clerks and to repeal\nsection 3207-a of the education law relating to authorizing the board\nof education of the city of New York to reduce the length of\nrecitation periods\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA : To provide the authority for enrolled\nstudents at least 17 years of age, upon consent from their school\ndistrict and parents (or legal guardians), to serve as election\ninspectors and poll clerks during elections.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS : Amends subdivision 6 of section\n3-400 and adds a new subdivision 7 to the election law to allow a\nperson who is 17 years of age who is properly enrolled in a school\ndistrict to be eligible to be appointed as an election inspector or\npoll clerk.\n\nAmends sections 132 and 143 of the labor law to exempt those\nparticipating as election inspectors or poll clerks on election day\nfrom the labor law which limits 17 year olds from working over 4 hours\na day on a school day and exempts participants from needing working\npapers.\n\nAdds a new section 2036 to the education law providing the authority\nfor school districts to allow 17 year old students to participate as\nelection inspectors and poll clerks at school district elections.\n\nAdds a new section 3207-a to the education law to allow 17 year olds\nto participate as election inspectors and poll clerks, upon parental\nconsent. Makes clear that those students participating as election\ninspectors or poll clerks shall be deemed as attending school on\nelection days.\n\nThe bill makes a technical change to the education law by repealing\nsection 3207-a of the education law that expired in 1959 and was never\nextended.\n\n JUSTIFICATION : Recruitment of election inspectors and poll clerks to\nwork our elections is a difficult task. For every statewide election,\n4, election inspectors or poll clerks are required per election\ndistrict polling station: roughly 60,000 people across the state to\nwork the election. In the November 2001 election, severe shortages of\npersonnel to work the election existed in several areas of the State.\nThis legislation would help reduce such widespread shortages while\nproviding an excellent hands-on learning experience of democracy in\naction for our state's young adults.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY : A.11173 (2002), Al252 (2003-04), A3006\n(2005-06). A.4059 (2007-2008).\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATION FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :  None.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE : This act shall take effect on the first of January\nnext succeeding the date it shall have become law.\n",
