    "lawCode": "Amd §3-400, El L; amd §§132 & 143, Lab L; amd §§2025, 2036 & 2607, rpld & add §3207-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5172A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the election law, the labor law, and the education\nlaw, in relation to authorizing persons seventeen years of age to\nserve as election inspectors and poll clerks and to repeal section\n3207-a of the education law relating to authorizing the board of\neducation of the city of New York to reduce the length of recitation\nperiods\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA :\nTo provide the authority for enrolled students at least 17 years of\nage, upon consent from their school district and parents (or legal\nguardians), to serve as election inspectors and poll clerks during\nelections.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nAmends subdivision 6 of section 3-400 and adds a new subdivision 7 to\nthe election law to allow a person who is 17 years of age who is\nproperly enrolled in a school district to be eligible to be appointed\nas an election inspector or poll clerk.\n\nAmends sections 132 and 143 of the labor law to exempt those\nparticipating as election inspectors or poll clerks on election day\nfrom the labor law which limits 17 year olds from working over 4 hours\na day on a school day and exempts participants from needing working\npapers.\n\nAdds a new section 2036 to the education law providing the authority\nfor school districts to allow 17 year old students to participate as\nelection inspectors and poll clerks at school district elections.\n\nAdds a new section 3207-a to the education law to allow 17 year olds\nto participate as election inspectors and poll clerks, upon parental\nconsent. Makes clear that those students participating as election\ninspectors or poll clerks shall be deemed as attending school on\nelection days.\n\nThe bill makes a technical change to the education law by repealing\nsection 3207-a of the education law that expired in 1959 and was never\nextended.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nRecruitment of election inspectors and poll clerks to work our\nelections is a difficult task. For every statewide election, 4,\nelection inspectors or poll clerks are required per election district\npolling station: roughly 60,000 people across the state to work the\nelection. In the November 2001 election, severe shortages of personnel\nto work the election existed in several areas of the State.  This\nlegislation would help reduce such widespread shortages while\nproviding an excellent hands-on learning experience of democracy in\naction for our state's young adults.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nA.11173 (2002), A.1252 (2003-04), A.3006 (2005-06), A.4059\n(2007-2008).\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATION FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
